## Nyx

A Unicorn, guardian of the ancient forest, entrusted one particular fey with a mission. Under the conveniently human-pronounceable nickname **Nyx**, she was to leave the Feywild and aid the mortals.

The Unicorn told her a simple truth.

> *"The evil the humans fight today may one day threaten our forest as well. Go into the mortal world and help them."*

The Unicorn never lied.

After all, it had watched mortals for longer than most kingdoms had existed, and one thing had remained remarkably consistent:

Humans are always fighting some great evil.

Whether it is a tyrant, a plague, an invading army, an ancient horror, or simply each other, there is always another battle. Sooner or later, whatever threatens them will eventually threaten everyone else.

What the Unicorn neglected to mention was that humanity was in no immediate danger of losing, that the forest itself was perfectly safe for the foreseeable future, and that helping humanity was not quite the primary motivation behind Nyx's mission.

The Unicorn's priorities were, roughly:

* Get Nyx out of the forest.
* Let her experience the world and, hopefully, grow into a wiser adult.
* Ensure that, should she inevitably cause another catastrophe, it happens somewhere that isn't the forest.
* Help the humans, if all of the above also happens to work out.

The Fey adore Nyx.

They simply find it much easier to adore her from several hundred miles away.

Nyx herself believes every word of her mission. She wants nothing more than to help. Give her a problem and she will attack it with boundless enthusiasm, limitless energy, and enough magical power to solve it several times over.

What she lacks is restraint.

Nyx does not stop to consider whether there might be unintended consequences. Nor does it occur to her that mortals routinely leave important details unsaid. Someone asks for help. Therefore, she helps—immediately, decisively, and with overwhelming force.

One story has become infamous.

A village was burning.

Someone shouted,

> *"Bring as much water as you can! Put out the fire!"*

Nyx immediately flew upstream and shattered the dam.

The fire vanished beneath a wall of water within moments.

So did much of the village.

The flood swept away homes, fields, bridges, livestock, and a distressing number of villagers.

When the survivors accused her of causing a tragedy, Nyx seemed genuinely confused.

To Nyx, the task had been completed perfectly. The fire had been extinguished quickly, completely, and with remarkable efficiency.

Over the years, mortals have given her many names.

*Chaos Incarnate.*

*Natural Disaster.*

*Walking Catastrophe.*

Nyx proudly treasures every one of them.

After all, humans only ever seem to invent a new nickname after she has successfully completed the task they asked of her.


